\begin{document}

\title{Maxwell--Jones Vector-Sensor Localization and Synchronization for
Distributed Near-Field RIS-Aided OFDM}

\author{Qingshan Gong,~\IEEEmembership{Member,~IEEE}}

\markboth{IEEE Transactions on Wireless Communications,~Vol.~XX, No.~XX, 2026}%
{Gong: Maxwell--Jones Vector-Sensor Localization and Synchronization}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We address 3-D localization and synchronization of a single-antenna user
in a wideband orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) uplink
observed by an electromagnetic vector-sensor (EVS) array only through
distributed near-field reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RISs). The
direct path is blocked, all cascaded paths share an unknown clock offset,
and gains and Jones coefficients are nuisances. A per-panel factorization
into space--frequency and calibrated Maxwell--Jones terms splits the
orthogonal projector and yields three exact consequences. First,
receiver selection only scales a panel's free-Jones Fisher information by
the retained polarization energy without altering its geometric structure,
and we characterize when two or fewer components lose panel separability.
Second, a parameter-independent projection compresses the receive dimension
from $96$ to $6$ as a pointwise likelihood isometry preserving that
information exactly. Third, the clock's unitary phase-ramp orbit renders the
conditional clock profile a finite trigonometric polynomial, globally
maximized by branch-and-bound with a deterministic optimality-gap
certificate, reducing the outer search from four dimensions to three.
At $-10$~dB the two-phase estimator attains a $0.31$-mm median position
error with $0.42\%$ position and $0.21\%$ clock catastrophic-event rates, against
$1.98\%$ for its closest in-model control and $9.79\%$--$99.9\%$ for three
adapted baselines at their own read-outs.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
reconfigurable intelligent surfaces (RIS), electromagnetic vector sensors
(EVS), near-field localization, clock synchronization, OFDM, polarimetric
sensing, variable projection, branch-and-bound, sufficient statistics,
Cram\'er--Rao bound, Fisher information.
\end{IEEEkeywords}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{E}{lectrically} large reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS)
panels place the user in the radiative near field, where spherical-wave
curvature carries range as well as
angle~\cite{wymeersch2020risloc,elzanaty2021bounds,cui2023nearfield,%
yang2024nfris}, so distributed panels can act as passive anchors for
joint 3-D localization and synchronization. An electromagnetic vector sensor
(EVS) adds a complementary observable: its co-located electric and magnetic
components map each two-dimensional Jones state to a calibrated six-component
Maxwell response~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent}. We study this
architecture when the direct link is blocked, several distributed RIS
panels are observed by a single shared EVS array, and all cascaded delays
share an unknown common clock offset. This setting creates three coupled
estimation difficulties.

The first difficulty is association. Distributed and multi-panel
near-field RIS methods, and the asynchronous formulations that admit an
unknown clock offset, typically rely on panel-resolved evidence before
joint estimation~\cite{ma2023distributed,fadakar2024multiris,liu2025multiris,%
fascista2022rissync,gong2024async,yan2025ris}. Here every passive path
reaches the same receive array within one observation, so a multilinear
decomposition returns unlabeled algebraic components, and a wrong label
assigns the wrong range, near-field response and clock replica at once.
Space--frequency evidence alone can be insufficient to label them, and
polarization is the missing observable: it enters RIS work as a
controllable beamforming resource~\cite{han2022dual,munawar2024dual} or as
an unknown recovered jointly with angle and
delay~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent,gong2026aimdf}, while
the closest cascaded near-field formulations remain scalar-sensor, modeling
the wideband XL-RIS channel without a clock~\cite{yang2024nfris} or
synchronizing through one SISO panel rather than through a clock shared
across distributed panels~\cite{yan2025ris}. Because the retained Maxwell
response is fixed by the calibrated RIS-to-BS direction, a calibrated EVS
supplies that discrimination without enlarging the physical aperture --
unless at most two components are retained, in which case
Section~\ref{sec:analysis} shows the separation can vanish outright.

The second difficulty is the observation dimension. Before Hankel
smoothing, full-EVS reception produces $6M_A\,N\,T$ complex samples, and an
unstructured reduction may distort the delay subspace, the profiled
likelihood or the localization information downstream of it. A useful
compression must provably preserve all three. Existing acquisition methods reduce this
burden either by multilinear factorization or by searching a discretized
near-field manifold~\cite{lin2021tensor,liu2025multiris,kolda2009tensor,%
sidiropoulos2017tensor,cui2022channel,bayraktar2024risloc,tipping2001sbl,%
li2024vbi,pati1993omp,fleury1999sage,yang2024nfris,zheng2025wideband}. We
keep their multilinear front end and coarse spherical search but not the
discretization: a fixed-size dictionary becomes increasingly inadequate as
the panel aperture grows, because the angular resolution sharpens with
aperture. Two constructions replace it: an exact adjoint
mapping into the element domain and a Rayleigh-spaced Fresnel beam-space
proposal, re-scored under the exact spherical criterion at a cost decoupled
from dictionary size (Section~\ref{subsec:beamspace}). The paraxial step
generates candidates only, so the acquisition carries no fixed-grid
resolution floor.

The third difficulty is the clock. It enters every cascaded delay inside
the same orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) phase, and the
blocked direct path removes the delay difference that would otherwise
cancel it, leaving a nonconvex joint problem whose numerical behavior
depends on the position--clock coordinate scaling. Variable projection (VP)
removes the linear nuisances~\cite{golub1973varpro}, but the semidefinite
route to a global solution~\cite{peng2025sdp} applies to timing models that
lift to a linear one, not to the complex OFDM likelihood here. The common
clock instead induces an exact phase-ramp orbit that leaves the Jones Gram
matrix invariant, so it can be profiled in the exact likelihood coordinates
with a deterministic optimality-gap certificate while the outer position
search stays 3-D. Our own EVS-OFDM estimator~\cite{gong2026aimdf} supplies
a multidimensional front end but none of the distributed geometry, panel
association, compression or clock profiling developed here. At low
signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) the dominant failure mode is a small
probability mass of gross wrong-basin solutions rather than uniform
variance inflation, so a local error bound alone cannot describe the
operating regime.

The key structural property is the per-panel factorization: the sensing
block of the $k$th RIS panel factorizes as
\begin{equation}
	\bA_k(\bchi)=
	\underbrace{\bc_k(\bchi)}_{\text{space--frequency geometry}}
	\otimes
	\underbrace{\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}}_{\text{retained Maxwell--Jones}},
	\label{eq:premise}
\end{equation}
where $\bchi=[\bp^{\mathsf T},\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}$ collects the unknown
position and common clock, $\bc_k$ carries all geometry and clock
dependence, and $\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$ is fixed
by the calibrated RIS-to-BS direction and receiver selection: calibrated
infrastructure fixes the polarimetric manifold of the sensing operator,
independently of $\bchi$. This one factorization acts on the three
difficulties in three different ways. Its Kronecker form splits the
orthogonal projector into a geometric and a polarimetric term, which
separates the polarization dependence of the Fisher information (C1); the
calibrated factor $\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$ confines every admissible
response to a receive subspace of dimension at most $2K$, which is what
makes a parameter-independent compression sufficient (C2); and confining
the clock to $\bc_k$ leaves it acting there as a single unitary phase ramp,
which is what makes its conditional profile certifiable (C3). Each is
stated as a theorem in Section~\ref{sec:analysis} and measured in
Section~\ref{sec:simulation}:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{C1: Maxwell--Jones information splitting}
(Theorem~\ref{thm:split}). Polarization enters the Fisher information as a
scalar, not as a geometry: each panel's free-Jones information is a
mode-independent geometric kernel weighted only by the polarization energy
its receiver retains. Separability is the part receiver choice can destroy,
and the theorem says exactly when: with at most two retained components and
a competing Jones block of full row rank, panels sharing a
space--frequency signature lose polarimetric separation entirely.
Simulations confirm that the distinction matters in practice: reducing the
receiver from six Maxwell components to one raises the catastrophic rate by
$13.6\times$, whereas the corresponding matched-PEB ratio is only
$5.7\times$.
\item \textbf{C2: Sufficient compression and reliable acquisition}
(Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}). A parameter-independent Maxwell--Kronecker
projection takes the receive dimension from $96$ to $6$ while leaving the
profiled likelihood pointwise unchanged and the free-Jones information
exactly equal. Applied ahead of delay recovery, the projection
improves the delay p95 error $35$-fold and cuts the catastrophic rate from
$2.29\%$ to $0.62\%$. Together with a Rayleigh-spaced beam-space search
and scale-profiled common-geometry fusion, it returns a physically labeled
initializer with no fixed-grid resolution floor.
\item \textbf{C3: Certified common-clock profiling}
(Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}). The clock's unitary phase-ramp orbit leaves the
Jones Gram matrix invariant, turning the conditional clock profile into a
finite trigonometric polynomial that branch-and-bound solves with a
deterministic optimality-gap certificate at every visited position. The
outer search drops from four dimensions to three and becomes invariant to
the clock unit. It holds the precision of a correctly scaled 4-D search at
$9$ rather than $13$ median outer evaluations, and every profile call in
the operating region returned a certificate within tolerance.
\end{itemize}

Together, C1--C3 give a two-phase estimator: Phase~I converts unlabeled
multilinear factors into a physically associated near-field initializer,
and Phase~II refines position and clock on the exact complex OFDM
likelihood. At $-10$~dB it reaches a $0.31$-mm median position error at a
$0.42\%$ catastrophic rate, against $1.98\%$ for its closest in-model
control and $9.79$--$99.9\%$ for three adapted literature baselines.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:system_model} develops the cascaded signal model and the
factorization it induces, Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} derives the two-phase
estimator, Section~\ref{sec:analysis} proves C1--C3, and
Section~\ref{sec:simulation} reports the numerical study.

Notation: bold symbols denote vectors/matrices; $(\cdot)^{\mathsf T}$,
$(\cdot)^{\mathsf H}$ and $(\cdot)^{\dagger}$ are transpose, Hermitian
transpose and pseudoinverse; $\otimes$ and $\odot$ are Kronecker and
Hadamard products; for a generic matrix $\bX$, $\bP_{\bX}=\bX\bX^{\dagger}$
and $\bPi_{\bX}^{\perp}=\bI-\bP_{\bX}$ are the orthogonal projectors onto
$\coll(\bX)$ and $\coll(\bX)^{\perp}$; and unqualified vector and matrix
norms are Euclidean and spectral, respectively.

\section{Maxwell--Jones Cascaded Signal Model}
\label{sec:system_model}

We consider a blocked line-of-sight (LoS) wideband uplink in which $K$
distributed reconfigurable intelligent surface (RIS) panels of
$M_R=M_{Rx}M_{Ry}$ elements each relay a single-antenna user equipment (UE)
to a base station (BS) carrying an $M_A$-element electromagnetic
vector-sensor (EVS) uniform linear array (ULA). The BS and RIS panels are
fixed, calibrated infrastructure, whereas the UE position, a common UE--BS
clock offset and the per-panel complex Jones coefficients are unknown. The
direct path is neglected under obstructed propagation, as is standard in
RIS-aided localization~\cite{fascista2022rissync,zhao2021timing}, so the
signal arrives only after reflection at the $K$ panels, which sweep $T$
known phase configurations. We parameterize each cascaded path so that
calibrated infrastructure and UE-dependent geometry occupy separate
Kronecker factors; that separation, collected in \eqref{eq:Ak}, is what
C1--C3 rest on.

Each electrically large panel forms a one-sided near-field cascade: the
UE--RIS hop retains the exact spherical wavefront, whereas the calibrated
RIS--BS hop is a center-frequency plane wave. For the reference panel
diagonal $D\simeq0.226$~m at $f_c=60$~GHz the panel Fraunhofer distance is
$20.5$~m, between the $3.6$--$4.2$~m UE--RIS ranges and the
$28.6$--$31.2$~m RIS--BS ranges. This one-sided convention is that of
XL-RIS channels~\cite{yang2024nfris,cui2022channel}, applied once per
panel.

The UE transmits pilots on $N$ subcarriers spaced by $\Delta f$, giving an
occupied bandwidth $B_{\rm occ}=(N-1)\Delta f$. Retaining $d_{\rm evs}$ of the six Maxwell components gives the
receive dimension $I=d_{\rm evs}M_A$, and the receive tensor
$\mathcal Y\in\C^{I\times N\times T}$ holds one such block per subcarrier
and configuration. Fig.~\ref{fig:geometry} shows the reference geometry.

\begin{figure}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{EVS_RIS_system_model-crop.pdf}
\caption{Reference geometry and cascaded-path parameterization.
(a)~Three oriented $64\times64$ RIS panels relay the blocked-LoS UE signal to
a $16$-element EVS ULA; only the RIS--BS links clear the panel Fraunhofer
distance. (b)~Local coordinates of the $k$th cascaded path
(Section~\ref{subsec:defchain}).}
\label{fig:geometry}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Calibrated Infrastructure Factor}
\label{subsec:calibrated}

Deployment calibration provides the BS phase center $\bp_B$, the RIS panel
centers $\br_k$ and rotations $\bR_k\in{\rm SO}(3)$, the element coordinates
$\brho_{k,m}\in\R^3$ in the $k$th panel frame, and the BS array and EVS
Maxwell manifolds. The RIS training codes $\bOmega_k\in\C^{T\times M_R}$ are
prescribed and likewise known, with constant-modulus entries implemented by
passive phase shifters, $[\bOmega_k]_{t,m}=e^{j\omega_{k,t,m}}/\sqrt{M_R}$,
as in RIS channel-estimation practice~\cite{lin2021tensor}. Elements are
centered on an
$M_{Rx}\times M_{Ry}$ grid of spacings $(d_x,d_y)$, the BS ULA lies along the
global $x$ axis, $\{\bm u_x,\bm u_y,\bm u_z\}$ are the global axes, and
$\kappa=2\pi/\lambda_c$ is the center-frequency wavenumber; explicit
coordinate and gauge conventions are in the supplement.

Consequently, the RIS--BS distance and propagation direction of panel $k$,
\begin{equation}
	d_{{\rm RB},k}=\|\bp_B-\br_k\|_2,
	\qquad
	\bs_{{\rm RB},k}=\frac{\bp_B-\br_k}{d_{{\rm RB},k}}
	\label{eq:rbgeom}
\end{equation}
are known before UE estimation. Because this hop lies beyond the panel
Fraunhofer distance, it contributes a plane-wave panel response and a BS
steering vector,
\begin{align}
	\big[\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\big]_m
	&=e^{-j\kappa\,\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bR_k\bs_{{\rm RB},k}},\nonumber\\
	\big[\bv_{{\rm B},k}\big]_i
	&=e^{j\kappa x_i\,\bm u_x^{\mathsf T}\bs_{{\rm RB},k}}.
	\label{eq:arb_vb}
\end{align}

The same calibrated propagation direction also fixes the polarization
manifold seen by the EVS array. A transverse plane wave along
$\bs_{{\rm RB},k}$ has Maxwell pair
$(\bE,\bH)=(\bq,\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bq)$ in impedance-normalized units, so
with the transverse basis
\begin{equation}
	\bq_{k,1}=\frac{\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bm u_z}
	{\|\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bm u_z\|_2},
	\qquad
	\bq_{k,2}=\bs_{{\rm RB},k}\times\bq_{k,1},
	\label{eq:qbasis}
\end{equation}
with a fallback axis near the pole (supplement), the six-component
vector-sensor
manifold~\cite{nehorai1994vector,khan2020sixcomponent} specializes to the
calibrated Maxwell--Jones block
\begin{equation}
	\bTheta_k^{(6)}=\begin{bmatrix}
	\bq_{k,1} & \bq_{k,2}\\[1pt]
	\bq_{k,2} & -\bq_{k,1}\end{bmatrix}\in\C^{6\times2},
	\label{eq:Theta}
\end{equation}
whose columns are orthogonal of squared norm two, so
$[\bTheta_k^{(6)}]^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k^{(6)}=2\bI_2$; any rotation of the
transverse basis is absorbed by the free Jones coefficient. A fixed
receiver selection
$\bS_{\rm mode}\in\R^{d_{\rm evs}\times6}$, with $d_{\rm evs}=1,2,6$ for the
scalar, dual-polarized, and full-EVS modes retaining respectively the first
electric component, the first two electric components, and all six, then
gives the retained block
$\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\triangleq\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}
\in\C^{d_{\rm evs}\times2}$.

Hence the calibrated receive block
$\bBm_k\triangleq\bv_{{\rm B},k}\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}\in\C^{I\times2}$
is fixed before UE estimation and carries no position or clock dependence;
only the associated complex Jones-domain coefficients $\bx_k$ remain
unknown. All UE dependence enters through the near-field wavefront and the
OFDM delay developed next.

\subsection{UE Geometry and Common-Clock Factor}
\label{subsec:defchain}

The unknown nonlinear parameter collects the UE position and the single
clock, $\bchi=[\bp^{\mathsf T},\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}
=[x,y,z,\Delta t]^{\mathsf T}\in\R^4$, with $\bp$ in a known service box
$\mathcal P$ and $\Delta t$ in a known interval $\mathcal T$ shorter than the
unambiguous delay period $1/\Delta f$. Each cascaded delay is a geometric
path length plus a single UE--BS clock offset shared by every panel, as in
asynchronous RIS
localization~\cite{fascista2022rissync,gong2024async,yan2025ris};
panel-dependent offsets~\cite{zhao2021timing} lie outside this model. With
the direct path blocked, no delay difference can eliminate $\Delta t$,
which therefore stays coupled to range in every cascade, and path-specific
constant phases are absorbed into the Jones coefficients.
Seen in the $k$th panel frame the UE is at $\bxi_k(\bp)=\bR_k(\bp-\br_k)$,
from which
\begin{equation}
	d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)=\|\bxi_k(\bp)\|_2,
	\qquad
	\bs_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)=\frac{\bxi_k(\bp)}{d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)}
	\label{eq:urgeom}
\end{equation}
with $d_{{\rm UR},k}=\|\bp-\br_k\|_2$ because $\bR_k$ is a rotation. The
first two components of $\bs_{{\rm UR},k}$ are the direction cosines
$(u_k,v_k)$, so the local parameterization searched in
Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} is
$\boldsymbol\eta_k=(d_{{\rm UR},k},u_k,v_k)$, in bijection with $\bxi_k$ on
the half-space in front of the panel.
The near-field dependence is captured by the RIS response,
\begin{equation}
	\bh_k(\bp) = \bOmega_k\,\bg_k(\bp),
	\qquad
	\bg_k(\bp) = \ba_{{\rm RB},k}\odot\ba^{\rm NF}_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp),
	\label{eq:hk}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
	\big[\ba^{\rm NF}_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp)\big]_m
	&= e^{-j\frac{2\pi}{\lambda_c}\delta_{k,m}(\bp)},\nonumber\\
	\delta_{k,m}(\bp)
	&= \big\|\bxi_k(\bp)-\brho_{k,m}\big\|_2
	- d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp),
	\label{eq:delta}
\end{align}
is the exact wavefront difference between element $m$ and the panel center.
Equation~\eqref{eq:delta} retains the exact spherical-wave phase across the
panel aperture; the Fresnel approximation enters only in Phase-I proposal
generation (Section~\ref{subsec:beamspace}).

Combining \eqref{eq:rbgeom} and \eqref{eq:urgeom}, the cascaded delay and
its OFDM phase ramp are
\begin{align}
	\tau_k(\bp,\Delta t)
	&= \underbrace{\frac{d_{{\rm UR},k}(\bp) + d_{{\rm RB},k}}{c_0}}_{
	\textstyle \tau_{g,k}(\bp)} + \Delta t,\nonumber\\
	\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)
	&= \big[\,e^{-j2\pi n\Delta f\,\tau_k(\bp,\Delta t)}\,\big]_{n=0}^{N-1}
	\in\C^N,
	\label{eq:dk}
\end{align}
with $c_0$ the speed of light. Spatial manifolds are evaluated at the carrier
$f_c$ while this delay phase is retained on every subcarrier; the neglected
spatial-wideband term peaks at $0.102$~rad in the reference configuration
(design condition and per-panel audit in the supplement).

The per-panel sensing block separates into three factors -- the RIS
training/near-field response, the OFDM delay ramp and the calibrated
Maxwell--Jones receive response -- whose Kronecker product gives the
premise~\eqref{eq:premise}:
\begin{equation}
	\bA_k(\bchi)
	= \bh_k(\bp)\otimes\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)\otimes\bBm_k
	= \bc_k(\bchi)\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})},
	\label{eq:Ak}
\end{equation}
where, by associativity of the Kronecker product,
$\bc_k(\bchi)=\bh_k(\bp)\otimes\bd_k(\bp,\Delta t)\otimes
\bv_{{\rm B},k}\in\C^{TNM_A}$ isolates the space--frequency factor. Pilots
are unit modulus and removed by pilot normalization; the scalar sample
expansion and the stacking order that reproduces \eqref{eq:Ak} are in the
supplement.

Equation~\eqref{eq:Ak}, which underlies every result below, admits two
useful groupings. The first, $\bh_k\otimes\bd_k\otimes\bBm_k$, exposes the
calibrated receive subspace: since $\bA_k\bx_k=\bh_k\otimes\bd_k\otimes(\bBm_k\bx_k)$, every
receive-mode fiber of an admissible panel response lies in the
$\bchi$-independent subspace $\coll(\bBm_k)$, of dimension at most two. The
second, $\bc_k\otimes\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$, separates the nonlinear
state from the receiver selection: all dependence on
$\bS_{\rm mode}$ is confined to $\bTheta_k^{({\rm mode})}$, whereas
$\bc_k(\bchi)$ is mode independent and carries the complete
position--clock dependence, with $\Delta t$ entering only through the OFDM
factor $\bd_k$. Section~\ref{sec:analysis}
turns these two groupings into the three guarantees.

\subsection{Structured Observation Model and Estimation Problem}
\label{subsec:obsmodel}

Stacking the panels as
$\bA(\bchi)=[\bA_1(\bchi),\ldots,\bA_K(\bchi)]\in\C^{M\times 2K}$ and
vectorizing $\mathcal Y$ in the same order gives
\begin{equation}
	\by = \bmu(\bchi,\bx) + \bn = \bA(\bchi)\,\bx + \bn,
	\qquad
	\bn\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf 0,\sigma^2\bI_M),
	\label{eq:obs}
\end{equation}
where $M=I\,N\,T$ and the noise is white across receive channels,
subcarriers and RIS configurations, with power $\sigma^2$ \emph{not}
provided to the estimator. The unknown Jones-domain coefficient of path $k$
is $\bx_k=\beta_k\be_k\in\C^2$, stacked as
$\bx=[\bx_1^{\mathsf T},\ldots,\bx_K^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}\in\C^{2K}$,
where the complex gain $\beta_k$ absorbs path loss, RIS efficiency and the
center-frequency phase of the cascade, and the unit Jones state
$\be_k=[\sin\gamma_k e^{j\psi_k},\ \cos\gamma_k]^{\mathsf T}$ carries
$\gamma_k\in[0,\pi/2]$ and $\psi_k\in(-\pi,\pi]$. Only the product
$\beta_k\be_k$ is identifiable, both factors are flat over the occupied
band, and the estimator treats $\bx_k$ as a free $\C^2$ nuisance.

Four assumptions close the model: panel phase shifts are scalar and
identical for the two polarizations, so a panel redirects a wave without
mixing its Jones components; the cyclic prefix covers the cascaded delay
spread, so the per-subcarrier OFDM model applies with residual
carrier-frequency offset and phase noise neglected; each panel contributes
one dominant cascaded component, so the model order equals the known panel
count $K$; and the $T$ configurations fall within one coherence block.

Profiling the unknown $\sigma^2$ concentrates the log-likelihood to a
strictly decreasing function of the residual energy, so maximum-likelihood
(ML) estimation of $(\bchi,\bx)$ is the least-squares problem
\begin{equation}
	\min_{\bp\in\mathcal P,\ \Delta t\in\mathcal T,\ \bx\in\C^{2K}}
	\big\|\by-\bA(\bp,\Delta t)\,\bx\big\|_2^2.
	\label{eq:ml_problem}
\end{equation}
The $2K$ Jones coefficients enter linearly and admit closed-form elimination,
whereas position and clock enter nonlinearly through \eqref{eq:delta} and
\eqref{eq:dk}. Nonconvex in four real coordinates, \eqref{eq:ml_problem} is
the data-fidelity term of both phases: Phase~I supplies a physically
associated initializer, and Phase~II minimizes it augmented by the
directional Jones penalty~\eqref{eq:penalized_short}
(Remark~\ref{rem:peb_reading}). An identifiability consequence follows: the
$K$ cascaded delays supply only $K$ constraints on the four unknowns, so at
$K=3$ range information alone leaves one direction unresolved, and the
near-field aperture supplies the missing local information through the
position-dependent phase slope and curvature of \eqref{eq:delta} across the
panel~\cite{chen2024nearfieldloc}; Section~\ref{sec:analysis} states the
local rank condition.

\section{Proposed Estimator: Sufficient Acquisition and Certified Clock Profiling}
\label{sec:algorithm}

Direct optimization of \eqref{eq:ml_problem} mixes four distinct roles in
one nonconvex problem: the physical panel permutation, the near-field
basin, the $2K$ linear Jones coefficients and the common clock. We
separate them structurally. Phase~I recovers shared-delay factors inside a
sufficient receive subspace, associates them with physical panels, and
enforces one common UE geometry; Phase~II eliminates the Jones
coefficients analytically and profiles the clock along its exact unitary
orbit. We call the two phases
\emph{Maxwell--Kronecker sufficient compression with common-geometry
initialization} (MKSC-GI) and \emph{common-clock orbit-profiled Jones
variable projection} (CCOP-JVP).

\subsection{Maxwell--Kronecker Factor Recovery}

\textit{1) Sufficient compression and the shared delay pole:}
Position and clock are entangled in every cascaded delay, so neither can be
estimated before the delays themselves. Smoothing the frequency axis of
$\mathcal Y$ into a Hankel pair with $P+L-1=N$ gives the data tensor
$\mathcal Z\in\C^{I\times P\times L\times T}$, on which two structural
facts of \eqref{eq:Ak} make delay recovery possible with no knowledge of
$\bchi$.

First, the receive dimension reduces without loss. Every receive fiber
lies in the calibrated plane $\coll(\bBm_k)$, so all admissible responses
lie in $\mathcal S_R=\sum_{k}\coll(\bBm_k)$, of dimension $r\le2K$, and the
orthogonal receive directions carry noise but no signal. With
$\bQ_S\in\C^{I\times r}$ an orthonormal basis of $\mathcal S_R$ obtained
from $\bPsi=[\bBm_1,\ldots,\bBm_K]$, only the EVS mode is compressed,
$\mathcal Z_{\rm d}=\mathcal Z\times_1\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}
\in\C^{r\times P\times L\times T}$, and $\bQ_S$ depends on calibrated
quantities alone.

Second, the delay of path $k$ reaches the observation only through the
single pole $z_k=e^{-j2\pi\Delta f\tau_k}$, so the compressed tensor is
quadrilinear,
\begin{equation}
	\mathcal Z_{\rm d}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}
	\ba_k\circ\bd_{P,k}\circ\bd_{L,k}\circ\bt_k+\mathcal N_{\rm d},
	\label{eq:sharedpole}
\end{equation}
with $[\bd_{P,k}]_p=z_k^{p}$ and $[\bd_{L,k}]_\ell=z_k^{\ell}$: one
physical delay generates \emph{both} frequency factors. With
$\bD_P=[\bd_{P,1},\ldots,\bd_{P,K}]$ and $\underline{\bD}_P$,
$\overline{\bD}_P$ denoting $\bD_P$ without its last and first row, that
single pole is exactly a shift invariance,
\begin{equation}
	\overline{\bD}_P=\underline{\bD}_P\,\boldsymbol\Phi,
	\qquad
	\boldsymbol\Phi=\diag(z_1,\ldots,z_K),
	\label{eq:shift}
\end{equation}
which subspace methods read off directly~\cite{roy1989esprit,gong2026aimdf}.
Under the pole-separation, smoothing-dimension and snapshot-rank conditions
of Section~\ref{sec:analysis}, the $K$ delays are thus identifiable from the
frequency axis alone.

\textit{2) Coupled factor recovery:}
Forward--backward total-least-squares estimation of signal parameters via
rotational invariance techniques
(ESPRIT)~\cite{roy1989esprit,gong2026aimdf} then recovers
$\boldsymbol\Phi$ of \eqref{eq:shift} from $\mathcal Z_{\rm d}$, each
estimated pole projected onto the unit circle. Once the
$\hat z_k$ are fixed, \eqref{eq:sharedpole} makes both frequency factors
known and every outstanding factor of \eqref{eq:Ak} enters linearly, so the
entire remaining recovery is one coupled least-squares solve rather than an
alternating iteration. Writing $\bZ_{rT,PL}\in\C^{rT\times PL}$ for the
unfolding of
$\mathcal Z_{\rm d}$ that groups the EVS and training modes against the two
frequency modes, and $\bD_\tau=[\bd_{P,k}\otimes\bd_{L,k}]_{k=1}^K$, the
coupled factors follow from
\begin{equation}
 \hat\bF=\bZ_{rT,PL}\bD_\tau^{\ast}
 (\bD_\tau^{\mathsf T}\bD_\tau^{\ast}+\lambda_\tau\bI)^{-1},
 \label{eq:coupledls_short}
\end{equation}
whose loading $\lambda_\tau$ is fixed in the supplement. Column $j$ of
$\hat\bF$ is reshaped to
$\C^{r\times T}$ and split by its leading singular pair into an EVS
space--polarization factor and an RIS training factor, the former lifted by
$\bQ_S$ to $\tilde\ba_j\in\C^{I}$ and the latter kept as
$\tilde\bt_j\in\C^{T}$, the ratio $\varrho_j=\sigma_{2,j}/\sigma_{1,j}$ of
that split retained as a per-column confidence used below to weight the
common-geometry fit.

\textit{3) Physical panel association:}
The recovered columns are algebraically unlabeled: nothing ties column $j$
to panel $k$, yet every downstream step needs that tie. Panel association is therefore
performed before any geometry is fused, by searching permutations under a
composite cost that combines three complementary cues: Maxwell-subspace
consistency identifies the panel-dependent receive manifold, spherical-wave
consistency tests the panel-local geometry, and the common-clock replicas
enforce a joint cross-panel constraint. For column $j$ and panel $k$,
\begin{equation}
 s_{j,k}=
 \frac{\|\bPi^{\perp}_{\bPsi_k}\tilde\ba_j\|}{\|\tilde\ba_j\|}
 +\min_{\boldsymbol\eta\in\mathcal E_k}
 \frac{\|\bPi^{\perp}_{\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)}\tilde\bt_j\|}
 {\|\tilde\bt_j\|},
 \label{eq:assoc_score}
\end{equation}
with $\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)$ the exact near-field panel response
\eqref{eq:hk} and $\mathcal E_k$ the local search box; both terms are
relative residual norms in $[0,1]$, so they combine without a calibration
constant. Writing $\hat d_{j,k}$ for the range coordinate of the minimizer
in \eqref{eq:assoc_score} and $\hat\tau_j$ for the delay of the estimated
pole, each pair also implies a clock replica
$\widehat{\Delta t}_{j,k}=\hat\tau_j-(\hat d_{j,k}+d_{{\rm RB},k})/c_0$,
and a permutation is scored by
\begin{equation}
 C(\pi)=\sum_{j=1}^{K}s_{j,\pi(j)}
 +\frac{w_{\rm clk}}{t_0}
 \big[\,\mathrm{sd}(\pi)+e_{\mathcal T}(\pi)\big],
 \label{eq:assoc_perm}
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{sd}(\pi)$ is the spread of the $K$ replicas
$\widehat{\Delta t}_{j,\pi(j)}$ about their mean, $e_{\mathcal T}(\pi)$ is
their mean excursion outside $\mathcal T$, $w_{\rm clk}=1/2$ and
$t_0=1$~ns. The labels are then
\begin{equation}
	\hat\pi=\arg\min_{\pi\in\mathfrak S_K}C(\pi).
	\label{eq:assoc_argmin}
\end{equation}
The three terms resolve complementary ambiguities: Maxwell consistency
supplies panel identity, spherical consistency supplies panel-local
geometry, and the shared-clock replicas couple all $K$ assignments at once,
so $C(\pi)$ does not reduce to a linear assignment. A coarse-to-exact
search over $\mathfrak S_K$ solves \eqref{eq:assoc_argmin} and returns an
assignment margin (supplement); its first tier is the one part of the
estimator whose cost grows factorially in $K$, negligible at $K=3$ but a
real limit on panel count.

\subsection{Beam-Space Acquisition and Common-Geometry Initialization}
\label{subsec:beamspace}

Labelled factors are still only training-domain vectors; the geometry they
encode must be read back out through the model of
Section~\ref{subsec:defchain}, one panel at a time, before the $K$ readings
can be reconciled into a single position. With $\tilde\bt_k$ the
training-domain factor that $\hat\pi$ assigns to panel $k$, its local
range--direction estimate minimizes
\begin{equation}
 J_{\rm RIS}(\boldsymbol\eta)
 =\|\bPi_{\bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)}^\perp\tilde\bt_k\|^2,
 \quad \bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)=\bOmega_k
 [\ba_{{\rm RB},k}\odot\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)],
 \label{eq:Jris_short}
\end{equation}
over $\boldsymbol\eta=(d_{{\rm UR},k},u_k,v_k)$ as parameterized in
Section~\ref{subsec:defchain}. A fixed-count angular dictionary of the polar
or beam-training kind~\cite{cui2022channel,zheng2025wideband} undersamples
large panels because its step is set by the search box while the panel
resolution $\lambda_c/D$ improves with aperture. Beam-space acquisition
moves the dominant angular search from the training domain to the element
domain, avoiding reliance on a fixed-count dictionary in the reference
large-aperture regime. Because the training code enters $\bh_k$ only on
the left, the numerator correlation of \eqref{eq:Jris_short} satisfies the
identity
\begin{equation}
 \bh_k(\boldsymbol\eta)^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k
 =\big[\ba^{\rm NF}(\boldsymbol\eta)\big]^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bg_k,
 \quad
 \tilde\bg_k=\ba_{{\rm RB},k}^{\ast}\odot
 (\bOmega_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k),
 \label{eq:adjoint_short}
\end{equation}
so one adjoint pass moves the factor into the element domain for every
subsequent trial geometry, an exact rewriting of the correlation and not an
inversion of $\bOmega_k$. Expanding \eqref{eq:delta} to second order about
the panel center separates the residual phase into a part linear in the
direction cosines $\bu$ and a curvature part set by the trial range $r$,
\begin{equation}
	\delta_{k,m}\simeq-\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu
	+\frac{\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2-(\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu)^2}{2r},
	\label{eq:fresnel}
\end{equation}
so the paraxial dechirp
$\tilde\bg_{k,r}[m]=\tilde\bg_k[m]e^{j\kappa\|\brho_{k,m}\|^2/(2r)}$
cancels the dominant curvature term and leaves a plane-wave sum
$e^{j\kappa\brho_{k,m}^{\mathsf T}\bu}$ over the element lattice. One
zero-padded two-dimensional discrete Fourier transform (2-D DFT) then
scores every direction cosine at once, on a grid that tracks the Rayleigh
limit at a cost decoupled from any dictionary size. Range resolves more
weakly than direction, so each visible angular peak receives an exact
spherical range scan before re-scoring by \eqref{eq:Jris_short} and local
refinement: \eqref{eq:fresnel} proposes candidates and never replaces the
spherical model.

Candidates are the union of the beam-space proposals and a fixed-count
dictionary, which retains coverage for panels small enough to reverse the
beam-space advantage; every member is judged by the same exact criterion
\eqref{eq:Jris_short}, so the candidate set changes acquisition coverage
and cost, not the forward model. Each panel-local fix
$\hat{\boldsymbol\eta}_k$ maps through the calibrated
panel pose to a global position $\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}$. These $K$ positions
generally disagree; the common-geometry stage replaces them by the single
position that best explains all $K$ training factors at once, enforcing a
common UE position across the physically associated panels.
Unknown complex factor scales are eliminated in the objective
\begin{align}
 J_{\rm geom}(\bp)
 &=\sum_{k=1}^K w_k\left[\|\tilde\bt_k\|^2-
 \frac{|\bh_k^{\mathsf H}(\bp)\tilde\bt_k|^2}{\|\bh_k(\bp)\|^2}\right]
 \label{eq:Jgeom_short}\\
 	 &=\min_{\balpha\in\C^K}\sum_{k=1}^K w_k
 \|\tilde\bt_k-\alpha_k\bh_k(\bp)\|^2,\nonumber
\end{align}
whose profiled scale
$\hat\alpha_k=\bh_k^{\mathsf H}\tilde\bt_k/\|\bh_k\|^2$ makes the two lines
identical, so $J_{\rm geom}$ sees only the component of $\tilde\bt_k$
orthogonal to $\coll(\bh_k)$ and is blind to the unknown path gains. The
weights $w_k=\max\{e^{-\varrho_k/\varrho_0},w_{\min}\}$ down-weight a column
whose rank-one split is poor while keeping every panel in the fit. A small
fixed set of bounded L-BFGS-B~\cite{byrd1995lbfgsb} starts minimizes
\eqref{eq:Jgeom_short}, built in deterministic order from the clipped
coordinate-wise median
$\hat\bp_0=\clip_{\mathcal P}\{\median_k\hat\bp_k^{\rm loc}\}$; the
constants are supplementary.

At the selected $\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$, one unregularized fixed-position clock
profile refreshes the Jones directions,
\begin{equation}
 (\hat{\Delta t}_{\rm ref},\hat\bx_{\rm ref})=
 \arg\min_{\Delta t\in\mathcal T,\bx}
 \|\by-\bA(\hat\bp_{\rm GI},\Delta t)\bx\|^2.
 \label{eq:refresh_short}
\end{equation}
With $\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}$ the $k$th two-entry block of $\hat\bx_{\rm ref}$,
the anchor is $\hat\be_k=\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}/\|\hat\bx_{{\rm ref},k}\|_2$,
re-estimated at the common position through the globally solved conditional
clock profile. These refreshed Jones directions initialize the Phase-II
directional regularizer.

\subsection{Common-Clock Orbit-Profiled Jones Variable Projection}
\label{subsec:ccop}

Phase~II returns to the exact criterion \eqref{eq:ml_problem} from the basin
just built. Its $2K$ Jones coefficients enter linearly and are eliminated in
closed form by variable projection~\cite{golub1973varpro},
leaving four nonlinear coordinates. The common clock is shared by all panels
and enters every cascaded delay in the same direction as range. It is also
the only nonlinear coordinate along which the likelihood admits an exact
unitary symmetry. Let
$\bA_0(\bp)\triangleq\bA(\bp,0)$ and
$\bD_t(\Delta t)=\diag([e^{-j2\pi n\Delta f\Delta t}]_{n=0}^{N-1})$.
Then
\begin{equation}
 \bU_t(\Delta t)=\bI_T\otimes\bD_t(\Delta t)\otimes\bI_I,
 \quad \bA(\bp,\Delta t)=\bU_t(\Delta t)\bA_0(\bp),
 \label{eq:orbit_short}
\end{equation}
and unitarity makes the Jones Gram clock invariant,
$\bA^{\mathsf H}\bA=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bA_0\triangleq\bG_0(\bp)$.
Phase~II uses a Gram-scaled block-diagonal penalty
\begin{equation}
 \bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)=\blkdiag_k\left[
 \lambda_k\tfrac12\tr\bG_{0,kk}(\bp)\,\bPi_{\hat\be_k}^{\perp}\right],
 \label{eq:Rdir_short}
\end{equation}
which penalizes departure of $\bx_k$ from the refreshed anchor direction
$\hat\be_k$ without freezing either coefficient. The dimensionless weight is
$\lambda_k=1$ throughout, so the penalty enters at the scale of the
per-path data Gram (Remark~\ref{rem:peb_reading}).

Phase~II accordingly minimizes
\begin{equation}
 J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx)=\|\by-\bA(\bp,\Delta t)\bx\|_2^2
 +\bx^{\mathsf H}\bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)\bx,
 \label{eq:penalized_short}
\end{equation}
whose first term is the nominal criterion \eqref{eq:ml_problem}. With
$\bH=\bG_0+\bR_{\rm dir}$ and
$\bb=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bU_t^{\mathsf H}\by$, the inner minimizer is
$\hat\bx=\bH^{-1}\bb$ and analytic Jones elimination gives
$F(\bp,\Delta t)\triangleq\min_{\bx\in\C^{2K}}J=\|\by\|^2-Q(\bp,\Delta t)$
with $Q=\bb^{\mathsf H}\bH^{-1}\bb$, hence the 3-D outer objective
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde F(\bp)=\|\by\|^2-
 \max_{\Delta t\in\mathcal T}Q(\bp,\Delta t).
 \label{eq:nested_short}
\end{equation}
Grouping the sufficient-statistic correlations by tone as
$\bb(\bp,\Delta t)=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}\bu_n(\bp)
e^{j2\pi n\Delta f\Delta t}$, the quadratic form through the
clock-independent $\bH^{-1}$ retains only tone differences $\ell=n'-n$.
Hence, for fixed $\bp$, $Q$ is a real trigonometric polynomial of degree at
most $N-1$,
\begin{equation}
 Q(\bp,\Delta t)=\sum_{\ell=-(N-1)}^{N-1}c_\ell(\bp)
 e^{j2\pi\ell\Delta f\Delta t},\quad c_{-\ell}=c_\ell^\ast,
 \label{eq:trig_short}
\end{equation}
so one factorization of $\bH$ and one zero-padded FFT supply all initial
clock candidates. With
$L_2=(2\pi\Delta f)^2\sum_\ell\ell^2|c_\ell|$, an interval centered at
$t_c$ with half-width $r_t$ obeys
\begin{equation}
 Q(t)\le Q(t_c)+|Q'(t_c)|r_t+\tfrac12L_2r_t^2.
 \label{eq:bound_short}
\end{equation}
Branch-and-bound maintains \eqref{eq:bound_short} over $\mathcal T$ and
returns an incumbent with
$Q(\Delta t^\star)-Q(\hat{\Delta t})\le U-Q(\hat{\Delta t})
\triangleq g_{\rm cert}$, where $U$ is the largest surviving interval upper
bound; subdivision stops once $g_{\rm cert}\le\epsilon_{\rm clk}$. Every
outer position therefore carries a certified inner maximizer, the guarantee
stated in Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}. The outer bounded search starts from
$\hat\bp_{\rm GI}$ and uses the envelope-theorem gradient of $Q$ where the
maximizing branch is unique and interior, with a derivative-free
safeguard~\cite{powell1964direct} otherwise. Tolerances, the safeguard
trigger and pseudocode for both phases are supplementary.


\section{Structural Analysis and Guarantees}
\label{sec:analysis}

The factorization \eqref{eq:premise} yields three exact structural
consequences: a Maxwell--Jones information split (C1), a sufficient
receive-subspace compression (C2), and an exact nested common-clock profile
with a deterministic certificate (C3). These consequences arise through
distinct mechanisms: its orthogonal projector splits into a geometric and a
polarimetric term, its calibrated Maxwell factor confines every admissible
response to a receive subspace of dimension at most $2K$, and its clock
dependence forms a single unitary factor. We first establish the free-Jones reference information and
the local identifiability condition used throughout.

\textit{A. Reference information and identifiability:}
Let $\bD_\chi=[\partial(\bA\bx)/\partial\chi_i]_{i=1}^4$. Eliminating the
unknown complex Jones coefficients from the white-Gaussian Fisher
information matrix gives the data-only free-Jones equivalent Fisher
information matrix (EFIM)
\begin{equation}
 \bJ_\chi^{\rm free}=\frac{2}{\sigma^2}
 \Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}.
 \label{eq:efim_short}
\end{equation}
Local position--clock identifiability after eliminating the Jones nuisance
requires
\begin{equation}
 \rank_{\R}[\Real\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}^{\mathsf T},
 \Imag\{\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}^{\mathsf T}]^{\mathsf T}=4,
 \label{eq:rankcond}
\end{equation}
which is exactly $\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\succ\mathbf0$, since
$\Real\{\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi\}$ is the Gram matrix
of that real stacking. When $\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\succ\mathbf0$, partitioning
its position and clock blocks,
$\bJ_p^{\rm eff}=\bJ_{pp}-\bJ_{pt}\bJ_{tt}^{-1}\bJ_{tp}$, gives the position
and clock error bounds (PEB, CEB),
${\rm PEB}=\sqrt{\tr[(\bJ_p^{\rm eff})^{-1}]}$ and
${\rm CEB}=\sqrt{[(\bJ_\chi^{\rm free})^{-1}]_{4,4}}$. With $K=3$, the cascaded delays supply at most three
independent constraints on the four position--clock coordinates and
therefore cannot identify $\bchi$ by themselves; in generic geometry one
direction remains, supplied by the near-field aperture, since after
complex-scale elimination the projected tangents $\bPi_{\bh_k}^{\perp}
[\partial\bh_k/\partial x,\partial\bh_k/\partial y,
\partial\bh_k/\partial z]$ retain the phase slope and curvature of
\eqref{eq:delta}. The scope is local and free-Jones: the pseudoinverse
projectors cover the rank-deficient Jones blocks that a scalar or dual mask
produces, gains and Jones directions are unknown deterministic nuisances,
and global panel-permutation and finite-bandwidth ambiguities lie outside
this rank test. Its proof is supplementary.

\textit{B. The Kronecker projector split:}
Both parts of C1 rest on one identity. Because $\bA_k$ is a Kronecker
product its Gram factors, $\bA_k^{\mathsf H}\bA_k=(\bc_k^{\mathsf H}\bc_k)
[\bTheta_k^{\mathsf H}\bTheta_k]$, and so does its projector; the
complement then splits into a term that is blind to geometry and a term
that is blind to polarization.

\begin{lemma}[Kronecker projector splitting]
\label{lem:kron}
Let $\bc\neq\mathbf0$ and $\bTheta\neq\mathbf0$ be arbitrary, with
$\bP_{\bTheta}=\bTheta\bTheta^{\dagger}$. With $\bA=\bc\otimes\bTheta$,
\begin{equation}
 \bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}
 =\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}+\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\otimes\bI,
 \qquad \bP_{\bc}=\frac{\bc\bc^{\mathsf H}}{\|\bc\|^2}.
 \label{eq:kronsplit}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{IEEEproof}
$\bA^{\mathsf H}\bA=(\bc^{\mathsf H}\bc)(\bTheta^{\mathsf H}\bTheta)$ and
$\bA^{\dagger}=\bc^{\dagger}\otimes\bTheta^{\dagger}$, so
$\bP_{\bA}=\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bP_{\bTheta}$. Expanding
$\bI=(\bP_{\bc}+\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp})\otimes(\bP_{\bTheta}
+\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp})$ and subtracting $\bP_{\bA}$ gives
\eqref{eq:kronsplit}. No rank assumption is used, so the identity survives
the component mask of C1(ii), which can make the retained Jones block rank
deficient.
\end{IEEEproof}

\textit{C. C1---Maxwell--Jones information splitting:}
\begin{theorem}[C1: Maxwell--Jones information splitting]
\label{thm:split}
\textbf{(i) Information consequence.} For one panel, write
$\bA=\bc\otimes\bTheta$, $\boldsymbol{\zeta}=\bTheta\bx$,
$\dot\bc_i=\partial\bc/\partial\chi_i$, and
$\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i=(\partial\bTheta/\partial\chi_i)\bx$. Then
\begin{equation}
 [\bD_\chi^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi]_{ij}
 =\|\boldsymbol{\zeta}\|^2\dot\bc_i^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bc}^\perp\dot\bc_j
 +\|\bc\|^2\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i^{\mathsf H}
 \bPi_{\bTheta}^\perp\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_j.
 \label{eq:split_short}
\end{equation}
Since calibrated infrastructure gives
$\partial\bTheta/\partial\bchi=\mathbf0$, the second term vanishes. Here and
in \eqref{eq:panelres_short}, $\bTheta=\bTheta_k^{(6)}$ is the \emph{full}
six-component block of \eqref{eq:Theta}, and $\bS_{\rm mode}$ ranges over
the candidate component selections of Section~\ref{sec:system_model}. The
mode retaining $\bS_{\rm mode}$ therefore yields, for a single panel and
whenever the retained signal is nonzero,
\begin{equation}
	\bJ_\chi^{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}\bJ_\chi^{\rm full},
	\quad
	{\rm PEB}^{\bS_{\rm mode}}
	=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}^{-1/2}\,{\rm PEB}^{\rm full},
	\label{eq:c1_scaling}
\end{equation}
with $\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}
=\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta\bx\|^2/\|\bTheta\bx\|^2$. Both statements are
single-panel; across panels the scaling becomes a panel-wise reweighting
rather than a global scalar, as treated after the theorem.

\textbf{(ii) Discrimination consequence.} For this comparison, define
$\bA_k^{(\bS)}(\bchi)=\bc_k(\bchi)\otimes
\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}$. If a competitor block matches panel $k$'s
space--frequency signature, i.e.
$\bc_{k'}(\bchi')\parallel\bc_k(\bchi)$, its free-Jones residual is
\begin{equation}
 \|\bPi_{\bA_{k'}^{(\bS)}(\bchi')}^\perp
 \bA_k^{(\bS)}(\bchi)\bx_k\|
 =\|\bc_k\|\,\|\bPi_{\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}^\perp
 \bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|.
 \label{eq:panelres_short}
\end{equation}
If the retained competitor block
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}\in\C^{d\times2}$ has full row rank for
$d\le2$, this residual is zero. With all six components it is at least
$\|\bc_k\|\|\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|\sin\theta_{\min}$, where
$\theta_{\min}$ is the minimum principal angle between the two panel
Maxwell subspaces.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
(i) A Jacobian column of $\bA\bx=\bc\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}$ is
$\bD_i=\dot\bc_i\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}
+\bc\otimes\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i$. Insert Lemma~\ref{lem:kron} into
$\bD_i^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_j$ and apply the mixed-product
rule. The summand $\bP_{\bc}\otimes\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}$ annihilates
$\dot\bc_i\otimes\boldsymbol{\zeta}$ and
$\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\otimes\bI$ annihilates
$\bc\otimes\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i$, so every cross term carries a
factor $\bPi_{\bc}^{\perp}\bc=\mathbf0$ or
$\bPi_{\bTheta}^{\perp}\boldsymbol{\zeta}=\mathbf0$; the two surviving
terms are \eqref{eq:split_short}. A mask replaces $\bTheta$ by
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta$ and leaves $\bc$ untouched, so with
$\dot{\boldsymbol{\zeta}}_i=\mathbf0$ the whole matrix is multiplied by
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$.

(ii) Apply \eqref{eq:kronsplit} to the competitor
$\bA_{k'}^{(\bS)}(\bchi')$ and evaluate it at
$\bc_k\otimes\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k$. Under
$\bc_{k'}\parallel\bc_k$ the term $\bPi_{\bc_{k'}}^{\perp}\otimes\bI$
annihilates its argument, leaving
$\bP_{\bc_{k'}}\otimes\bPi^{\perp}_{\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}$ and
hence \eqref{eq:panelres_short}. If
$\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}\in\C^{d\times2}$ has row rank $d\le2$ its
range is all of $\C^{d}$ and that projector is zero. For $d=6$, let $\bU$
and $\bV$ be orthonormal bases of the two Maxwell subspaces; from
$\sigma_{\max}(\bU^{\mathsf H}\bV)=\cos\theta_{\min}$ every nonzero
$\bz\in\coll(\bTheta_k^{(6)})$ obeys
$\|\bPi_{\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}\bz\|\le\cos\theta_{\min}\|\bz\|$ and therefore
$\|\bPi_{\bTheta_{k'}^{(6)}}^{\perp}\bz\|\ge\sin\theta_{\min}\|\bz\|$.
\end{IEEEproof}

C1 says that polarization enters the geometry of the problem as a scalar.
Component selection cannot rotate the information eigenvectors of a panel:
it can only rescale that panel's contribution by the fraction
$\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}$ of polarization energy it retains, which is
\eqref{eq:c1_scaling}. Across panels the scaling is no longer
global, since $\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}$ built on the full
$\bA=[\bA_1,\ldots,\bA_K]$ factors into per-panel projectors only when the
blocks and their projected tangents are mutually orthogonal. In the
approximately block-orthogonal regime it reads
$\bJ_\chi^{\rm free}\approx\frac{2}{\sigma^2}\sum_k
\|\bS_{\rm mode}\bTheta_k^{(6)}\bx_k\|^2\bG_k$, with
$[\bG_k]_{ij}=\Real\{\dot\bc_{k,i}^{\mathsf H}\bPi_{\bc_k}^\perp
\dot\bc_{k,j}\}$ selection independent, so each panel keeps a different
fraction of its energy and a receiver choice reweights panels unevenly
instead of rescaling the aggregate. Part (ii) is a statement of a different kind: not a local perturbation but
a wrong-panel hypothesis distance, which collapses to zero for $d\le2$ while
the single-panel information of (i) merely rescales. Local information and
cross-panel discrimination are therefore separate casualties of receiver
choice, and only the first is visible to a local bound.

\textit{D. C2---Sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression:}
\begin{theorem}[C2: Sufficient Maxwell--Kronecker compression]
\label{thm:compress}
Let $\bU_S=\bI_{NT}\otimes\bQ_S$ be the semi-unitary lifting matrix in the
vectorization of \eqref{eq:obs}, and suppose
$\coll[\bA(\bchi)]\subseteq\coll(\bU_S)$ for all admissible $\bchi$. Write
$\by_c=\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\by$ and
$c_\perp=\|(\bI-\bU_S\bU_S^{\mathsf H})\by\|^2$. Under the matched
white-Gaussian model:
(i) for known $\sigma^2$, $\by_c$ is sufficient for $(\bchi,\bx)$;
(ii) for unknown $\sigma^2$, the pair $(\by_c,c_\perp)$ is sufficient for
$(\bchi,\bx,\sigma^2)$, and $\by_c$ alone still preserves the maximizer in
$(\bchi,\bx)$ of the $\sigma^2$-profiled likelihood, because
\begin{equation}
 \|\by-\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2
 =\|\by_c-\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA(\bchi)\bx\|^2+c_\perp
 \label{eq:isometry_short}
\end{equation}
and $c_\perp$ does not depend on $(\bchi,\bx)$;
(iii) $\by_c$ alone preserves the profiled optimizer, the parameter
derivatives and the free-Jones EFIM \eqref{eq:efim_short} exactly;
(iv) the noiseless delay poles are preserved provided the compressed
non-frequency snapshot matrix
$[\beta_k(\bh_k\otimes\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}\bBm_k\be_k)]_{k=1}^K$ has rank $K$,
which holds generically for nondegenerate panel responses and training when
$rT\ge K$ but is checked rather than inferred from dimension alone.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
Complete $\bU_S$ to a unitary $[\bU_S,\bU_S^{\perp}]$. Then
$\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\by\sim\mathcal{CN}(\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA\bx,\sigma^2\bI)$
and $(\bU_S^{\perp})^{\mathsf H}\by\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf0,\sigma^2\bI)$
independently, the second mean vanishing by the assumed subspace inclusion.
For known $\sigma^2$ the second density does not involve $(\bchi,\bx)$,
which is (i); for unknown $\sigma^2$ it depends on the discarded block only
through the scalar $c_\perp$, so the factorization theorem gives (ii).
Because $\bA=\bU_S\bU_S^{\mathsf H}\bA$, the cross term in the orthogonal
decomposition of $\by$ about $\coll(\bU_S)$ vanishes, which is exactly
\eqref{eq:isometry_short}; its second term is data only. Writing
$\bA=\bU_S\bA_c$ and $\bD_\chi=\bU_S\bD_{\chi,c}$, semi-unitarity gives
$\bPi_{\bA}^{\perp}\bD_\chi=\bU_S\bPi_{\bA_c}^{\perp}\bD_{\chi,c}$,
pseudoinverses included, and taking the Gram of both sides leaves
\eqref{eq:efim_short} unchanged, proving (iii). For (iv),
$\bQ_S^{\mathsf H}$ acts on the EVS mode alone and therefore commutes with
both Vandermonde frequency factors, so the noiseless pole set is unchanged
and shift-invariance identifiability survives whenever the compressed
snapshot matrix has column rank $K$; each of its $K\times K$ minors is real
analytic in the training phases, Jones states and nonzero gains, so if one
feasible design makes a minor nonzero its zero set has measure zero.
\end{IEEEproof}

C2 separates statistical sufficiency from finite-sample algorithmic
conditioning. Equation~\eqref{eq:isometry_short} is a pointwise identity,
not an approximation, and its discarded term $c_\perp$ carries no
dependence on $(\bchi,\bx)$, so no candidate changes its score relative to
any other and the profiled optimizer and free-Jones EFIM are preserved
exactly; the projection meanwhile removes $I-r$ noise-only receive
directions and cuts residual and Jacobian rows by $I/r$. Phase~I is an
ESPRIT-based subspace procedure rather than the exact ML optimizer, so that
removal can materially improve its finite-sample recovery without
contradicting the isometry. The sufficiency and EFIM statements require the
matched white-Gaussian model and the stated subspace inclusion; pole
preservation additionally requires Theorem~\ref{thm:compress}(iv).

\textit{E. C3---Certified common-clock profiling:}
\begin{theorem}[C3: Certified common-clock profiling]
\label{thm:clock}
Assume $\bH(\bp)=\bG_0(\bp)+\bR_{\rm dir}(\bp)\succ\mathbf0$.

\textbf{(i) Exact nesting.} Analytic Jones elimination and global inner
clock maximization nest the penalized criterion \eqref{eq:penalized_short}
exactly:
$F(\bp,\Delta t)=\min_{\bx}J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx)=\|\by\|^2-Q(\bp,\Delta t)$,
\begin{equation}
 \min_{\bp\in\mathcal P}\widetilde F(\bp)
 =\min_{\bp\in\mathcal P,\,\Delta t\in\mathcal T,\,\bx\in\C^{2K}}
 J(\bp,\Delta t,\bx),
 \label{eq:nestval_short}
\end{equation}
and the two argument sets correspond, $\arg\min_{\bp}\widetilde F$ being the
projection onto $\bp$ of $\arg\min_{\bp,\Delta t,\bx}J$, so pairing any outer
minimizer $\bp^\star$ with a global inner clock maximizer of
$Q(\bp^\star,\cdot)$ and with
$\bx^\star=\bH^{-1}(\bp^\star)\bb(\bp^\star,\Delta t^\star)$ recovers a joint
minimizer.

\textbf{(ii) Unit invariance.} The outer profile is invariant under every
invertible affine clock-unit reparameterization with $\mathcal T$
transformed accordingly.

\textbf{(iii) Certified clock solution.} The interval bound
\eqref{eq:bound_short} guarantees at every visited position
\begin{equation}
 Q(\Delta t^\star)-Q(\hat{\Delta t})\le g_{\rm cert}
 =U-Q(\hat{\Delta t}),
 \label{eq:cert_short}
\end{equation}
and $g_{\rm cert}\le\epsilon_{\rm clk}$ whenever termination occurs by the
prescribed tolerance rather than the subdivision cap.
\end{theorem}

\begin{IEEEproof}
(i) By \eqref{eq:orbit_short} the orbit map $\bU_t$ is unitary, so
$\bH=\bA_0^{\mathsf H}\bA_0+\bR_{\rm dir}$ carries no clock. For fixed
$(\bp,\Delta t)$, $J=\|\by\|^2-2\Real\{\bb^{\mathsf H}\bx\}
+\bx^{\mathsf H}\bH\bx$ is therefore a strictly convex quadratic in $\bx$
with unique minimizer $\bH^{-1}\bb$ and minimum $\|\by\|^2-Q$. The inner
minimum being attained at every $(\bp,\Delta t)$, minimization may be
carried out in any order, which is \eqref{eq:nestval_short}, and the
correspondence of argument sets follows from that attainment. (ii) An
invertible affine change of clock unit is a bijection of $\mathcal T$ onto
its image, so the attained value set of $Q(\bp,\cdot)$, hence
$\widetilde F$, is unchanged. (iii) Termwise differentiation of
\eqref{eq:trig_short} gives $|Q''|\le(2\pi\Delta f)^2
\sum_\ell\ell^2|c_\ell|=L_2$ uniformly on $\mathcal T$, so Taylor's theorem
about an interval center yields \eqref{eq:bound_short}. The solver keeps a
finite cover of $\mathcal T$ and discards an interval only when its upper
bound falls below the incumbent, so no discarded interval can contain a
maximizer and the largest surviving bound obeys $Q(\Delta t^\star)\le U$.
This proves \eqref{eq:cert_short} under any stopping rule, so the gap stays
valid even if the subdivision cap is reached; only the tolerance test
depends on the cap.
\end{IEEEproof}

The certificate covers the one-dimensional clock solve and removes its
outer coordinate; the 3-D position search remains local. The corresponding
clock-unit and certificate controls are reported in
Section~\ref{subsec:res_c3}.

\begin{remark}[Scope of the free-Jones reference bound]
\label{rem:peb_reading}
The frozen weight $\lambda_k=1$ does not taper with $\sigma$, so the
free-Jones PEB is a reference for the unpenalized data model and is not
claimed as a proven bound for the penalized estimator. A sufficient -- not
necessary -- tapering condition under which the two coincide is
supplementary; the $\lambda$-sweep of Section~\ref{subsec:res_c1} measures
the gap directly.
\end{remark}

\textit{F. Complexity:}
Each Phase~II outer position
evaluation costs $O(qM_r+q^2(N+T))+O(q^2N^2)+O(qM_c\log M_c+q^3)
+C_{\rm cert}$, with $q=2K$, $M_r=rNT$, and $M_c$ the zero-padded clock-FFT
length. The profiled solve therefore acts on $M_r$ compressed rows instead
of the $M=I\,N\,T$ raw rows. Because the Kronecker structure of
\eqref{eq:Ak} makes each Gram block a cached position-independent
Maxwell--Jones factor times one length-$N$ and one length-$T$ inner
product, the Gram update costs $O(q^2(N+T))$ rather than $O(q^2M_r)$. The
term-by-term derivation is supplementary; measured runtime and memory are
reported in Section~\ref{subsec:res_cost}.
\section{Numerical Results}
\label{sec:simulation}

\subsection{Protocol and Reference Bounds}
\label{subsec:res_setup}

Unless varied, the matched scene uses $f_c=60$~GHz, $K=3$ oriented
$64\times64$ half-wavelength RISs, an $M_A=16$ EVS ULA, $N=63$
subcarriers at $\Delta f=5$~MHz, and $T=256$ training blocks, with the UE at
$(1.25,0.55,0.75)$~m and a true clock of $5$~ns in a $[0,10]$-ns search
interval; complete geometry and signal parameters are supplementary. Path
gains and Jones states are drawn independently per trial, and SNR is the
average pre-noise sample SNR on the active receive entries,
${\rm SNR}=10\log_{10}[\|\bmu_{\rm act}\|_2^2/(M_{\rm act}\sigma^2)]$, with
absolute path loss absorbed into this normalization; the receiver-mode
study instead fixes $\sigma^2$ from the full-EVS observation and reuses it
across masks. Phase~I acquires the basin when it returns a finite
$\hat\bp_{\rm I}$ with $\|\hat\bp_{\rm I}-\bp^\star\|_2\le0.1$~m. The
declared operating region is ${\rm SNR}\ge-10$~dB; results quoted at
$-15$ or $-20$~dB are below it and are labelled as such.

Each accuracy cell contains $480$ paired trials, except the external
benchmark of Section~\ref{subsec:res_benchmark}, which uses $960$, the C3
factor control at $100$ and the serialized cost cells at $30$; methods in
one cell share the scene, RIS codes, Jones states, gains and noise
realization, while different suites use independent seed roots. A position
estimate is catastrophic if the method fails or its error exceeds $0.1$~m,
a clock estimate if its absolute error exceeds $1$~ns. Rate intervals are exact $95\%$
Clopper--Pearson, p95 and p99 are nearest-rank, paired differences use
bootstrap intervals, and outlier exchanges use McNemar's exact test; all
hyperparameters, every baseline included, were fixed before the reported
cells were evaluated. Each fixed-SNR study is measured at $-10$~dB except
the model-order and RIS element-phase sweeps at $0$~dB. Suites use
independent seed roots and their rates are never pooled; the cross-suite
audit is supplementary.

At $-10$~dB, over $20$ Jones/gain realizations at the default position, the
median free-Jones PEB and CEB are $0.404$~mm and $3.35$~ps; oracle- and
free-Jones PEBs differ by under $0.005\%$, so the data-only bound is the
sole reference, and since the EFIM scales as $2/\sigma^2$, any CEB quoted
at another SNR is this median scaled by $\sigma$. The complete bound table
is supplementary.

\subsection{External Benchmark}
\label{subsec:res_benchmark}

\begin{figure*}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{fig_benchmark.pdf}
\caption{External benchmark, as-published tier, $960$ shared-realization
trials per SNR, comparing the proposed MKSC-GI+CCOP-JVP, range-scaled 4-D
Jones-VP, Algebraic CPD with joint mapping, VBI/SBL stopped at its own
closed-form fusion, and near-field OMP--SAGE--WLS methods. The top and
bottom rows report position and clock metrics, respectively; the three
columns show in-basin precision, error-distribution tails, and
catastrophic-event rates. (b),~(e)~Complementary cumulative distribution functions
(CCDFs) at $-10$~dB; the dashed line marks
the $0.1$-m and $1$-ns thresholds, so each crossing is that method's rate
in~(c) and~(f). (d)~The CEB reference is the median bound of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_setup} scaled by $\sigma$. Rate intervals are exact
Clopper--Pearson; the dotted line is one trial in $960$, hollow markers on it
being exact zeros.}
\label{fig:benchmark_combined}
\end{figure*}

Five methods run on shared realizations under an as-published protocol
(Fig.~\ref{fig:benchmark_combined}): every route is stopped at the
continuous read-out its own cited formulation prescribes -- the algebraic
canonical polyadic decomposition (CPD) of~\cite{lin2021tensor}, solved by
generalized-eigenvalue simultaneous diagonalization, polished by alternating
least squares~\cite{kolda2009tensor} and read out in the CP-factor domain;
near-field orthogonal matching
pursuit (OMP) through space-alternating generalized
expectation--maximization (SAGE) sweeps and a weighted least-squares
read-out~\cite{yan2025ris}, and variational Bayesian inference with
sparse Bayesian learning (VBI/SBL) through its native closed-form
fusion~\cite{li2024vbi} --
and no comparator receives the proposed route's exact-model Phase~II
polish. Every number below is a property of the evaluated adaptation to
this common forward model, not of the reference method itself; sources,
grids, caps, stopping rules and a refinement-matched VBI/SBL diagnostic
separating its acquisition stage from its read-out are supplementary.

The comparison against the closest in-model control isolates the
contribution of the proposed route. At $-10$~dB it attains a $0.310$-mm median
position error at a $0.42\%$ catastrophic rate, whereas range-scaled 4-D VP
matches the median ($0.312$~mm) but not the tail ($1.98\%$). The advantage
is therefore in reliability, not in in-basin precision.

The three adapted baselines separate more sharply once each is stopped at
its own read-out. VBI/SBL's closed-form fusion gives a $26.2$-mm median at
a $9.79\%$ catastrophic rate, the Algebraic CPD adaptation is catastrophic on
$45.4\%$ of trials, and near-field OMP fails on $99.9\%$ with a $1.2$-m
median. Each retains an SNR-independent floor over $-5$ to $30$~dB: the
VBI/SBL median stays at $26.1$--$26.5$~mm, set by its native read-out
rather than by resolution; the Algebraic CPD catastrophic rate stays at
$34$--$37\%$; and the near-field OMP adaptation fails on essentially every
trial, its far-field direction-acquisition stage failing to bracket a basin
in this blocked-LoS, deep-near-field scene.

These gaps are statistically resolved. McNemar's exact test on the paired
catastrophic indicator rejects equality against every comparator
($p\le1.5\times10^{-3}$), with $21$ exchanges against range-scaled 4-D VP
and $98$, $432$ and $955$ against VBI/SBL, Algebraic CPD and near-field OMP, all
but the first entirely one-sided. The advantage is nonetheless confined to
acquisition: from $-5$~dB upward the proposed route has no catastrophic
trial, matched by range-scaled 4-D VP only from $0$~dB, and above $0$~dB
the two coincide to a few micrometers.

Synchronization mirrors the position ranking: at $-10$~dB the
clock-catastrophic rates are $0.21\%$ for the proposed route against
$1.56\%$, $6.15\%$, $7.92\%$ and $88.9\%$ for range-scaled 4-D VP,
Algebraic CPD, VBI/SBL and near-field OMP, and at $0$~dB the proposed clock RMSE
($1.08$~ps) sits at a $1.06$-ps CEB reference, a descriptive comparison and
not a bound test (Remark~\ref{rem:peb_reading}). The range-scaled 4-D route
matches that inlier precision; the adapted baselines carry
$8.1\times10^{-3}$--$0.11$ normalized mean-square error among their own
inliers, a property of the geometries they acquire. The ordering survives
outside the declared region: at $-15$ and $-20$~dB the proposed route keeps
catastrophic rates of $1.25\%$ and $5.00\%$ against $6.88\%$ and $22.08\%$
for range-scaled 4-D VP, the lowest of any evaluated route at both points,
while near-field OMP is catastrophic on every trial. The full below-region
sweep is supplementary.

\begin{figure*}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.68\textwidth]{fig_receiver.pdf}
\caption{C1 information and efficiency, $480$ paired trials per cell at a
common absolute noise level, verifying Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(i).
(a)~Matched accuracy (solid) against the free-Jones PEB (dashed).
(b)~Paired inlier-conditional efficiency ratio with its $95\%$
paired-bootstrap interval ($4\,000$ replicates, trials resampled in pairs);
the shaded band is $\pm2\%$ and the intervals are wider than it.}
\label{fig:c1_receiver}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{C1: EVS Information and Panel Separability}
\label{subsec:res_c1}

\textit{Information.} The splitting identity of
Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(i) holds to numerical precision: single-panel
EFIMs reproduce
$\bJ_\chi^{\bS_{\rm mode}}=\alpha_{\bS_{\rm mode}}\bJ_\chi^{\rm full}$ to
$5.7\times10^{-16}$ (audit supplementary). Its consequence is measurable:
at $0$~dB the scalar, dual and full modes reach conditional RMSEs of
$0.815$, $0.330$ and $0.133$~mm, tracking the matched PEB order of
Fig.~\ref{fig:c1_receiver}(a).

\textit{Separability.} Discrimination changes by more than information
alone predicts. At $-10$~dB the three modes carry catastrophic rates of
$14.17\%$, $2.29\%$ and $1.04\%$ -- a $13.6\times$ scalar/full ratio
against a $5.7\times$ matched-PEB ratio -- and the excess is the
panel-label loss of Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(ii).
Fig.~\ref{fig:c1_separability} isolates that mechanism. At $1$~ns
cascaded-delay separation, full EVS resolves a panel pair with probability
$0.87$--$0.88$ across the four tested Jones overlaps: their largest spread
is $1.7$ percentage points, below the $4.6$-point Clopper--Pearson
half-width of a single cell, so the sweep resolves no dependence on
overlap. The dual-polarized and scalar modes instead collapse toward zero
as the overlap grows, spanning $82.7$ and $61.9$ points. Formal trend tests
over overlap are supplementary.
This is \eqref{eq:panelres_short} in the data: with $d\le2$ retained
components the competing Jones block spans $\C^{d}$ and the residual
separating the two panels can vanish outright, whereas six components hold
it above $\sin\theta_{\min}$. Delay separation limits every mode
alike; only the reduced modes are additionally limited by polarization.

\textit{Efficiency.} For the two Jones-resolving modes the RMSE/PEB ratio
is $0.98$--$1.02$ across two independently seeded suites, consistent with
efficiency once the $\pm6\%$ paired-bootstrap intervals are accounted for;
those intervals resolve no ordering between them, and their separation from
scalar sensing in Fig.~\ref{fig:c1_receiver}(a) tracks the SNR-independent
PEB ratios $5.7$ and $2.3$. The scalar mode's high-SNR floor (near
$0.098$~mm at $30$~dB) belongs to the rank-one structure of
single-component sensing and persists identically at $\lambda_k=0$: a
penalty sweep over $\lambda_k\in\{0,0.1,1,10\}$ exchanges no catastrophic
trial and moves inlier RMSE by at most $2$ parts in $10^5$, so the
separation above is receiver driven rather than a property of the
regularizer. Full sweeps and bootstrap tables are supplementary.

\begin{figure*}[!tb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.80\textwidth]{fig_resolvability.pdf}
\caption{Polarimetric panel separability at $-10$~dB, the mechanism of
Theorem~\ref{thm:split}(ii): resolution probability against cascaded-delay
separation at four Jones overlaps with exact Clopper--Pearson bands, for
(a)~scalar, (b)~dual-polarized and (c)~full-EVS reception. The dashed
vertical line marks the reference scene's minimum separation and the boxed
number the largest spread over overlap. Reducing the retained Maxwell
components makes separability overlap dependent; the full-EVS spread stays
within the sampling resolution of a single cell.}
\label{fig:c1_separability}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{C2: Sufficient Compression and Reliable Acquisition}
\label{subsec:res_c2}

\textit{Effect of sufficient compression on acquisition reliability.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:compress} makes the projection free in exact-likelihood
terms, and the experiment confirms that it is: from $0$~dB upward the
compressed and raw routes are numerically coincident. What the projection
changes is the subspace geometry that Phase~I's ESPRIT front end actually
sees. At $-10$~dB the leakage ratio $\sigma_{K+1}/\sigma_K$ falls from
$0.822$ to $0.353$, the delay p95 error improves $35\times$
($4.87\to0.141$~ns), basin acquisition rises from $97.50\%$ to $99.38\%$
and the catastrophic position rate falls from $2.29\%$ to $0.62\%$. The
isometry therefore does not alter the exact-likelihood accuracy; what it
permits is discarding $90$ of $96$ receive dimensions before a subspace
method is applied to the remainder.

\textit{Contribution of the individual Phase~I mechanisms.} Table~\ref{tab:c2_ladder}
separates the three Phase~I mechanisms on a nested ladder at $-20$~dB, a
stress point below the declared operating region chosen because the tail
there is large enough to resolve them. Compression alone removes
three quarters of the catastrophic mass ($21.46\to5.62\%$) by denoising the
delay poles; common-geometry fusion, by turning $K$ disagreeing
panel-local fixes into one position, is what converts that into basin
capture, raising acquisition from $78.12\%$ to $96.04\%$ and cutting the
remaining tail by a further $30\%$; and anchor refresh leaves the rate
untouched while lowering reconstruction NMSE by $27\times$. None of this is
restart diversity: a seven-start pool and an oracle-position start both
land within one trial of the proposed rung.

\textit{Aperture scaling.} Growing the panel
from $16^2$ to $64^2$ elements lowers conditional RMSE from $1.66$ to
$0.42$~mm while holding the catastrophic rate in $[0.21,0.62]\%$, so the
estimator realizes the aperture gain that motivates near-field operation.
Collecting it is an acquisition property, not a refinement property: the
proposed Phase~I holds a basin rate of $99.2$--$99.6\%$ across that
sixteenfold change in element count, whereas the baseline Phase~I ranges
from $14.58\%$ at $M_R=16^2$ to $95.62\%$ at $64^2$ and never reaches the
proposed rate at any tested aperture. Accuracy improves monotonically with
$M_A$, $T$ and bandwidth, and at $K=4$ the proposed and range-scaled 4-D
routes carry catastrophic rates of $1.88\%$ and $12.92\%$; complete sweeps
are supplementary.

