\subsection{C3: Clock Profiling and Certification}
\label{subsec:res_c3}

Table~\ref{tab:c3_control} isolates Phase~II at $-10$~dB by giving four
routes the same $100$ MKSC-GI initializers, so the rows differ only in how
the clock coordinate is carried. CCOP-JVP preserves the $0.509$-mm,
$3.44$-ps precision of a correctly range-scaled 4-D search while removing
one nonlinear coordinate, cutting the median outer evaluation count from
$13$ to $9$, while eliminating sensitivity to the clock coordinate's
units: the
explicit 4-D route depends on whether the clock is carried in seconds,
nanoseconds or range units, the two mis-scaled labels losing one trial in
$100$ that the profiled route keeps and the seconds label degrading
conditional position RMSE by $31\%$, whereas CCOP-JVP returns
bit-identical output under all three parameterizations, as
Theorem~\ref{thm:clock}(ii) requires.

The certification condition is satisfied throughout the tested operating
region. In the independently seeded internal suite, every clock profile the
estimator solved returned a valid gap: across its $4\,320$ operating-region trials and the $35\,833$
profile calls they made, the gap normalized by its tolerance has median
$0.69$ and maximum $0.9997$, so the subdivision cap
was never reached, the clock branch was never ambiguous and the Powell
safeguard never fired. This certifies the inner optimum only; the outer 3-D
search remains local, and indeed 4-D VP and CCOP-JVP sharing one Phase~I
initializer have identical catastrophic rates at every SNR of the
independent suite, which is why the tail suppression of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_c2} is attributed to C2 and not to C3.

