\subsection{Deployment Boundaries and Spatial Generalization}
\label{subsec:res_robustness}

Three sensitivities set the deployment envelope, one per panel of
Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}; the remaining mismatch and generalization sweeps
and the audit of the frozen Phase~I constants are supplementary.
\textit{Calibration} is the binding one
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(a)--(b)): RIS--BS angular error degrades
precision long before capture,
raising conditional RMSE from $0.447$ to $31.2$~mm at $0.25^\circ$ while
the basin rate holds at $99.17\%$, and capture itself collapses only at
$1^\circ$ ($59.58\%$). Receiver gain, phase and sensor-position mismatch,
RIS element-phase error and first-order colored noise are all milder over
their tested ranges.

\textit{Model order} is a material assumption rather than a tuning
parameter (Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(c)). In the dedicated $0$-dB sweep, $\widehat K=2$ is structurally
destructive ($0.710$-m median, $P_{\rm cat}=65.4\%$), and $\widehat K=4$
and $5$ keep submillimeter medians while carrying $35.4\%$ and $49.0\%$
catastrophic rates -- which is also why median-only robustness reporting
would mislead in either direction.

\textit{Delay coincidence} bounds acquisition where information does not
(Fig.~\ref{fig:boundaries}(d)). Across a $5\times5\times2$ position grid the proposed median per-position
catastrophic rate is $0.62\%$ against $2.92\%$ for 4-D VP, and both hard
corners lie near a cascaded-delay coincidence surface, the worst reaching
$31.25\%$ at a minimum separation of $0.062/B_{\rm occ}$ -- while the
free-Jones EFIM stays full rank at every tested position, corners included.
Local information sufficiency therefore does not imply global acquisition
reliability: deployment screening needs a delay-coincidence check and a
coarse global fallback, not a PEB map alone. Every sweep here holds one
infrastructure layout and samples no panel-placement diversity.

\subsection{Cost, Training Budget, and Failure Diagnostics}
\label{subsec:res_cost}

In the serialized single-thread benchmark at $-10$~dB the proposed route
requires $2.19$~s and $2.52$~GB peak resident set size: faster than Algebraic CPD
($3.41$~s) and near-field OMP--SAGE--WLS ($11.1$~s), slower than
range-scaled 4-D VP ($1.59$~s) and VBI/SBL's native read-out ($0.92$~s),
the fastest tier but the one that pays the floor of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_benchmark}. An independently seeded $30$-trial
decomposition agrees with that total to $5\%$ and places the dominant cost
in the exact spherical panel projection ($1.12$~s) rather than in certified
clock profiling ($0.25$~s). Per-method memory and the full component
breakdown are supplementary.

The training budget is set by mobility rather than by bandwidth. With
$\Delta f=5$~MHz a $25\%$ cyclic prefix is $50$~ns, covering the $19.1$-ns
sum of cascaded-delay spread and clock-search interval, so one
configuration per symbol gives $T_{\rm train}\simeq64~\mu$s at $T=256$,
excluding a reconfiguration latency that dominates the payload beyond about
$0.25~\mu$s per switch. At $60$~GHz and $1$~m/s the UE moves $64~\mu$m per
sweep, already $16\%$ of the median PEB, the two becoming equal near
$6$~m/s: the block-constant model covers pedestrian mobility at the
reported accuracy and is exhausted at vehicular speed, where $T$ must be
cut against the aperture-driven acquisition gain of
Section~\ref{subsec:res_c2}. Full EVS costs $96$ calibrated receive chains.

The remaining failures are confined to Phase~I and are identified by the
basin diagnostic. Across the $4\,320$ operating-region trials, exactly five
proposed-route trials are catastrophic, and exactly those five fail that
diagnostic, each with a Phase~I delay RMSE above $0.5$~ns; panel
association is correct in $99.93\%$ of trials overall and in every trial at
and above $0$~dB. Every remaining failure in the tested region therefore
originates in acquisition rather than in refinement.
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We studied joint 3-D localization and synchronization in a blocked-LoS
distributed near-field RIS uplink observed by one shared EVS array, and
showed that a single algebraic fact organizes the problem: because the
per-panel sensing block is a Kronecker product of a space--frequency factor
and a calibrated Maxwell--Jones factor, its orthogonal projector splits.
From that split follow an exact information factorization in which
polarization acts as a scalar weight and a separability condition telling
us when a reduced receiver loses panel labels outright (C1); a
parameter-independent $96\to6$ compression that is a pointwise likelihood
isometry and preserves the free-Jones information exactly (C2); and a
unitary clock orbit that converts the conditional clock into a finite
trigonometric polynomial, solved to a deterministic optimality-gap
certificate and removing one dimension from the outer search (C3).

Empirically the gain is concentrated in basin reliability. Once the
proposed route and a correctly scaled 4-D Jones-VP baseline reach the same
basin their inlier accuracies coincide; the catastrophic tail is what
separates them, and it is traced to Phase~I, where EVS discrimination,
compressed delay recovery, physical panel association and common-geometry
fusion act. CCOP-JVP contributes a lower-dimensional, clock-unit-invariant
refinement with a certified inner solve, which does not globalize the
nonconvex outer search.

Three boundaries remain. RIS--BS angular calibration limits
millimeter accuracy before it affects capture; the panel count is a
material model-order assumption; and full-rank local information does not
preclude acquisition failure near cascaded-delay coincidence surfaces.
Spatial-wideband effects, direct leakage and diffuse multipath, and
panel-placement diversity lie outside the present model and call for
explicit extensions -- spatial-wideband and multipath modeling, model-order
selection, and coarse global screening of coincidence regions -- rather
than retuning of this estimator.

